Diagnostic: what each draw was aimed at, and what was in the texture it read

Measurement-only.  The traces are on the d3d11 channel; the two dumps are off
unless their environment variable is set.

  device.c   `altars-draw`, `altars-rt`, `altars-viewport`, `altars-scissor`,
             `altars-srv` -- every draw, and the render target, viewport,
             scissor and shader resources in force when it was issued.
             `scripts/d3d-draw-map.py` replays a log and puts them back
             together.  `altars-copy` prints CopySubresourceRegion's source
             box, which is what let a glyph tile's fill be matched to the copy
             that carries it.

  view.c     `altars-srvres` -- the size of the resource behind a shader
             resource view, the counterpart of 0009's `altars-rtv`, so a draw's
             textures can be identified by what they are rather than by
             pointer.

  shared.c   `d3d_texture2d_dump` writes any texture out through a staging copy
             on its own device's context.  0016 can only dump a surface that is
             shared, because that is the only one this file already has in
             system memory; the texture Office composites from is not shared
             and has no render target view, so it needed its own way out.

  device.c   `WINE_ALTARS_DUMP_CACHE=N` dumps the destination of every Nth
             CopySubresourceRegion; `WINE_ALTARS_DUMP_BOX="l,t,r,b"` dumps both
             sides of the copy whose source box is exactly that, with the
             source texture's size, misc flags, device, shared state,
             generation and dirty flag.

What they found, in the order they found it, is the reason to keep them:

  * Office composites the window out of a **2048x2048 texture that has no
    render target view** -- 320 draws into the 1438x808 swapchain, all with the
    full-window viewport, 62 of them with a scissor overlapping the navigation
    rail, all sampling that one texture.  So the rail was not a region Office
    declines to draw; it was a region drawn from an empty part of a cache.
  * That cache is filled by **2006 CopySubresourceRegion calls per start-up**,
    all of them while the calling thread holds the shared surface, sourcing
    from the three 2048x1280 keyed-mutex atlases.
  * Matching each glyph fill's rectangle against those copies' source boxes
    showed Office **does** copy the rail's tiles -- 开始 is filled at
    (130,3)-(154,14) and copied from (129,0)-(155,16).
  * And dumping both sides of that copy showed the source texture empty with
    `gen 0`, which named the bug in 0018 exactly.

The dumps write PPM and PGM -- colour and the alpha plane separately -- for the
reason 0016 records: these surfaces are premultiplied BGRA, so black text at
full coverage is (0,0,0,255) and a colour-only dump cannot tell it from
"nothing was drawn here".

